Thumbs.db
.DS_Store
*.prenav
*.bak

# Restore bundles hold WireGuard private keys, Wi-Fi passphrases and the
# console's write password. They must never reach a public repository.
backups/
*.tar.gz
*.packages

# Live router state, in case a copy is ever pulled off the device into this tree
# to look at. DENY BY DEFAULT under etc/, then re-admit the tracked files by name.
#
# This block used to be a list of directories to deny, and a list of names goes
# stale the moment anything is added — which it did, inside a single session:
# repeater-known (Wi-Fi passphrases) had to be remembered by hand, while oui and
# nlbw-daily were simply missed, and so was every /etc/config file. The worst of
# those is etc/config/network, which is where WireGuard private keys live, with
# etc/config/wireless holding the Wi-Fi passphrases beside it.
#
# Naming the dangerous directories was still the wrong SHAPE, and that is what
# this rewrite fixes. Denying three subtrees left the SSH host keys and
# authorized_keys, the root password hash, the uhttpd TLS key, the iOS pairing
# records and both traffic-accounting databases all trackable — every one of them
# present on a live router — along with any directory nobody has thought of yet.
# One `git add -A` after copying a file in to look at it would have published
# them, irreversibly, to a public repository.
#
# So the rule is inverted rather than extended. Everything under etc/ is private;
# a file becomes committable only by being named below. Adding a directory to a
# deny list is what failed twice now, so it is no longer possible to fail that
# way: a path nobody anticipated is private by default, not public by default.
#
# ADDING A CONSOLE FILE UNDER etc/? It will not be staged until you give it a `!`
# line here. That is the point. The old form failed by publishing silently; this
# one fails by leaving out a file you can see is missing.
#
# `!etc/**/` IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT DECORATION. Git never descends into an excluded
# directory, so without it every exception below is dead — the parent is already
# excluded and the re-inclusion can never be evaluated. Verified rather than
# assumed: with the exceptions but no `!etc/**/`, `git add -A` over a tree holding
# an SSH host key, a shadow file and a TLS key stages nothing whatsoever — not
# the secrets, and not the console files it was supposed to keep.
#
# THE DENY IS UNANCHORED (`**/etc/`) ON PURPOSE. Root-anchored `etc/**` kept the
# promise above only at the repo root: extract a backup bundle into a
# subdirectory to inspect it — the very scenario this block names — and the
# whole tree landed OUTSIDE the guard, admitted. An etc/ at any depth is now
# private. The directory re-admit and the exceptions stay root-anchored,
# deliberately: an extraction tree gets no exceptions, so git never even
# descends into one.
**/etc/**
!etc/**/
!etc/crontabs/root
!etc/dashboard/classmap.example
!etc/hotplug.d/iface/12-console-services
!etc/hotplug.d/iface/15-travel-dns
!etc/hotplug.d/iface/31-tethering-clash
!etc/hotplug.d/iface/32-pbr-uplink
!etc/hotplug.d/iface/33-uplink-width
!etc/hotplug.d/iface/34-vpn-resume
!etc/hotplug.d/iface/35-nlbw-v6prefix
!etc/hotplug.d/iface/99-repeater-iot
!etc/hotplug.d/net/30-tethering
!etc/hotplug.d/net/40-rrm-neighbors
!etc/hotplug.d/usb/40-usbmuxd
!etc/init.d/beryl-vpndns
!etc/init.d/cpugovernor
!etc/init.d/pingmon
!etc/sysctl.d/99-local.conf

# The push topic is effectively a shared secret - anyone who knows it can read
# the router's notifications. It lives on the router only.
etc/dashboard/notify.conf
